We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$.
The mochi are arranged in ascending order of size, so $A_1 \leq A_2 \leq \dots \leq A_N$.
We can form a kagamimochi by placing mochi A on top of mochi B if the size of A is at most half the size of B. That is, if $size(A) \leq \frac{1}{2} size(B)$.
We need to choose two mochi from the given $N$ mochi. Let's say we choose mochi with index $i$ and mochi with index $j$ (where $1 \leq i, j \leq N$).
The problem statement says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same."
This implies that we are counting pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that we can form a valid kagamimochi.
